WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.214
Can a loop recover one value?

00:00:02.214 --> 00:00:04.134
Take the function omega squared.

00:00:04.134 --> 00:00:08.464
Our target is its value at x equal to one half, namely one quarter.

00:00:08.464 --> 00:00:12.496
Cauchy's formula recovers this number from a counterclockwise circuit.

00:00:12.796 --> 00:00:14.451
Place a pole at the target.

00:00:14.451 --> 00:00:17.160
Divide the function by omega minus x.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:21.810
The denominator vanishes at one half, while the numerator there is one quarter.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.602
This gives a genuine simple pole.

00:00:23.902 --> 00:00:25.494
Start with a small circle.

00:00:25.494 --> 00:00:26.859
Its centre is x.

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:30.527
Take its radius epsilon to be one quarter for the drawing.

00:00:30.527 --> 00:00:36.778
Any sufficiently small positive radius works if the circle and its interior lie inside the ellipse.

00:00:37.078 --> 00:00:39.566
The tangent cancels the denominator.

00:00:39.566 --> 00:00:44.194
On the circle, omega minus x is epsilon times e to the i t.

00:00:44.194 --> 00:00:48.695
The differential contributes i times that same factor times d t.

00:00:48.695 --> 00:00:54.903
After dividing by two pi i, only the function value times d t over two pi remains.

00:00:55.203 --> 00:00:56.752
Keep the constant part.

00:00:56.752 --> 00:00:58.757
Expand the square along the circle.

00:00:58.757 --> 00:01:02.810
One term is constant, one turns once, and another turns twice.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:06.500
Both rotating terms average to zero over a full turn.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.081
What survives is x squared, or one quarter.

